<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=torpedo">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torpedo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Whitehead torpedo, fish torpedo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A torpedo is a self-propelled underwater weapon with an explosive warhead that detonates on contact with or close to its target. The word first named mines and spar torpedoes, used by Cornelius Drebbel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fulton">Robert Fulton</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. In 1864 the naval officer Giovanni Luppis gave the English engineer Robert Whitehead plans for a floating weapon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a>, now Rijeka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>; Whitehead made from it the first modern self-propelled torpedo, run underwater by compressed air, and presented it to the Austrian Imperial Naval commission on 21 December 1866. On 16 January 1878 the Turkish steamer Intibah became the first ship sunk by self-propelled torpedoes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torpedo.jpg" alt="Torpedo" width="330" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bidgee · CC BY-SA 3.0 au · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mark_8_torpedo_in_Germanton_Park.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word torpedo first named electric rays, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> torpēdō, meaning lethargy or sluggishness. In naval usage the American inventor David Bushnell was reported to have used it for his submarine, and this probably inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fulton">Robert Fulton</a>, who used it for his stationary mines. Historically the term covered primitive naval mines and spar torpedoes, used ad hoc until the late 19th century, and from about 1900 it has been used strictly for self-propelled underwater explosive devices. Around 1280 the engineer Hasan al-Rammah, who worked for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, described a rocket-propelled surface craft carrying an explosive payload, which historians classify as a surface-skimming rocket rather than an underwater missile.</p><p>The first prototype of a self-propelled torpedo came from a commission by Giovanni Luppis, an Austro-Hungarian naval officer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a>, and Robert Whitehead, an English engineer who managed a factory there. In 1864 Luppis gave Whitehead the plans of the Salvacoste, a floating weapon steered from the land by ropes, which the naval authorities had dismissed because its steering and propulsion were impractical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Whitehead torpedo of 1866, the first successful self-propelled torpedo, used compressed air stored at pressures of up to 2.55 MPa to drive a piston engine turning a single propeller. It travelled about 180 metres at an average speed of 6.5 knots. The Austrian government invested in it, and by 1870 improved devices could run about 900 metres at up to 6 knots. In 1871 the British Admiralty paid Whitehead £15,000 for certain developments. In 1906 his torpedoes covered nearly 1,000 metres at an average of 35 knots. Rivals included the wire-driven Brennan torpedo, the flywheel-driven Howell torpedo and John Ericsson's electric torpedo of 1873.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> frigate HMS Shah first fired a self-propelled torpedo in anger at the Battle of Pacocha on 29 May 1877, against the Peruvian ironclad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>, which outran it. On 16 January 1878 the Turkish steamer Intibah became the first vessel sunk by self-propelled torpedoes, launched from torpedo boats under Stepan Osipovich Makarov during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a>. Torpedoes allowed small boats to destroy large ships without big guns. In the First World War German submarine torpedoes disrupted supply lines to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and in May 1915 a torpedo from SM U-20 sank the Lusitania off Ireland in eighteen minutes, killing nearly 1,200 civilian passengers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Torpedo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Explosions in its <b>torpedo</b> compartment sent the nuclear submarine Kursk to the seabed, killing all 118 crew.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Confederate submarine rammed a <b>torpedo</b> into the Union warship off Charleston, the first sinking of an enemy ship by a submarine.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Japanese <b>torpedo</b> boats struck the Russian Pacific squadron at night, opening the Russo-Japanese War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-8">8 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Crippled by a <b>torpedo</b> hit to her rudder, Bismarck was pounded by British battleships and sank in the Atlantic.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-27">27 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1866</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Fiume (Rijeka, Croatia)</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Robert Whitehead · Giovanni Luppis · <a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Around 1280 Hasan al-Rammah described a rocket-driven incendiary craft that moved across the water, often cited as an early torpedo, but historians class it as a surface-skimming rocket; until about 1900 the word also covered mines and spar torpedoes.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=torpedo">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
